<v Alex>Big shakeup at Meta's data centers.

16
00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:49.230
<v Alex>And CoreWeave's stock just jumped a lot.

17
00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:49.980
<v Alex>Let's get into it.

18
00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:53.130
<v Maya>Time for The Ticker.

19
00:00:53.130 --> 00:01:00.930
<v Maya>At Wednesday's close, the S&amp;P 500 finished at 7,619, up 1.3 percent.

20
00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:06.740
<v Maya>The Nasdaq Composite was up 1.6 percent and the Dow was up 0.5 percent.

21
00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:11.090
<v Alex>And in big tech, Tesla had the best day, up 2.9 percent,

22
00:01:11.090 --> 00:01:15.560
<v Alex>while Microsoft was at the other end, down 2.3 percent.

23
00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:17.230
<v Alex>That's a snapshot, not advice.

24
00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:23.690
<v Alex>Anduril is claiming they’ve demonstrated a satellite that can track spacecraft even

25
00:01:23.690 --> 00:01:26.270
<v Alex>when they’re in Earth’s shadow.

26
00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:27.950
<v Alex>Ars Technica has the story.

27
00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:29.210
<v Maya>Wait, really?

28
00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:29.950
<v Maya>Tracking spacecraft?

29
00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:31.510
<v Alex>Yeah, apparently.

30
00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:38.310
<v Alex>It's their satellite, Anduril-216, orbiting about 590 kilometers up.

31
00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:41.630
<v Maya>That’s a lot of hardware for what sounds like a niche problem.

32
00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:44.790
<v Alex>It's a tech demo, funded internally.

33
00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:48.210
<v Alex>They launched it on a SpaceX rideshare mission back in July.

34
00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:50.550
<v Maya>And what exactly is it doing up there?

35
00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:56.070
<v Alex>It's got an infrared imager that works during those dark periods—about a third of

36
00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:57.220
<v Alex>each orbit.

37
00:01:57.220 --> 00:01:59.230
<v Alex>They’re using it to spot other objects.

38
00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:05.290
<v Maya>So, just an edge-compute processor, power management, and this infrared sensor.

39
00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:06.670
<v Maya>Seems like a focused payload.

40
00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:08.260
<v Alex>Exactly.

41
00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:11.310
<v Alex>They’re saying it can see maneuvers others might be trying to hide.

42
00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:16.400
<v Maya>So, TSMC, the biggest chipmaker around,

43
00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:20.500
<v Maya>is putting a billion dollars into OpenAI and Anthropic.

44
00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:22.110
<v Maya>That’s the surprising part, isn't it?

45
00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:25.350
<v Alex>I thought they were just happily selling chips to those companies.

46
00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:30.890
<v Maya>They’ve already made a ton of money supplying chips for AI data centers.

47
00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:36.910
<v Maya>This investment is up to 500 million in OpenAI and another 500 in Anthropic.

48
00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:38.480
<v Alex>That's a lot.

49
00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:43.330
<v Alex>What's the breakdown – preferred stock for OpenAI, common shares for Anthropic?

50
00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:44.410
<v Maya>Yep.

51
00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:46.580
<v Maya>Apparently, the board approved it all.

52
00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:50.930
<v Maya>It's a bit of a move to give the AI market a boost, according to Ars Technica.

53
00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:54.280
<v Alex>I wonder how much say they’ll actually have.

54
00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:57.550
<v Alex>Are they just throwing money or expecting something in return?

55
00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:02.510
<v Maya>Analysts are concerned it makes it harder to see what’s actually driving demand for

56
00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:07.410
<v Maya>AI hardware and services, you know, with this kind of circular investment.

57
00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:09.790
<v Alex>Circular financing…

58
00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:11.970
<v Alex>yeah, I can see why that's tricky to value.

59
00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:12.670
<v Maya>It is.

60
00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:19.020
<v Alex>Maya, did you see this TechCrunch piece about Anthropic’s IPO?

61
00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:22.590
<v Alex>Seems like the founders are all taking a pretty significant payday.

62
00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:26.090
<v Maya>$250 million each, apparently.

63
00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:29.960
<v Alex>Yeah, each selling just over three million shares.

64
00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:32.770
<v Alex>It’s interesting they’re all selling the same amount, though.

65
00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:36.250
<v Maya>It does suggest some level of coordination.

66
00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:42.050
<v Maya>They’ll still own about 3.2% of Class A shares each, collectively 22%.

67
00:03:42.064 --> 00:03:43.190
<v Alex>They’re still in control?

68
00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:48.370
<v Maya>They’ll have 51.1% of the votes, thanks to Class F stock.

69
00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:52.030
<v Maya>Amazon and Google also have shares, but of a different class.

70
00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:57.830
<v Alex>So, they left OpenAI to focus on AI safety, and now they’re getting rich.

71
00:03:58.290 --> 00:03:59.610
<v Maya>That’s the story, yes.

72
00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:02.100
<v Maya>Reed Hastings wants in too, apparently.

73
00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:09.630
<v Maya>OpenAI's letting businesses create AI agents to represent them within ChatGPT.

74
00:04:09.695 --> 00:04:10.930
<v Alex>Like, full-on brand reps?

75
00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:11.810
<v Maya>Apparently.

76
00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:16.450
<v Maya>The Verge reports it’s starting as a beta program for e-commerce and online

77
00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:17.969
<v Maya>services in the US.

78
00:04:17.969 --> 00:04:19.190
<v Alex>That's a lot of businesses.

79
00:04:19.990 --> 00:04:24.110
<v Maya>Businesses can specify instructions and context for these agents,

80
00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:26.130
<v Maya>so they can answer questions about their products.

81
00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:28.490
<v Alex>So, comparison shopping, basically?

82
00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:32.190
<v Maya>That’s the main use case they're highlighting, yes.

83
00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:34.670
<v Maya>And there's this thing called Business Hub for verification.

84
00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:36.710
<v Alex>Verification?

85
00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:37.790
<v Alex>How do they even do that?

86
00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:43.610
<v Alex>This one caught my eye because it’s a pretty significant shift in Meta’s

87
00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:48.690
<v Alex>infrastructure leadership – their head of infrastructure, Santosh Janardhan,

88
00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:49.210
<v Alex>is leaving.

89
00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:50.320
<v Maya>Leaving?

90
00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:51.450
<v Maya>Seriously?

91
00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:53.410
<v Maya>That’s a lot of experience walking out the door.

92
00:04:53.910 --> 00:04:57.550
<v Alex>Apparently he’s handing over the reins to David Brown,

93
00:04:57.550 --> 00:05:00.250
<v Alex>who just joined from AWS in July.

94
00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:03.440
<v Maya>Hold on, so he’s only been there a few months?

95
00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:04.470
<v Maya>That’s a quick transition.

96
00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:10.180
<v Alex>Yeah, Janardhan will support Brown through the first quarter of 2027,

97
00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:11.690
<v Alex>so it's not cold turkey.

98
00:05:11.690 --> 00:05:15.450
<v Alex>CNBC reports he said it's “the right time.”

99
00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:16.110
<v Maya>Right.

100
00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:20.970
<v Maya>And this is all part of Mark Zuckerberg’s push to expand data center operations,

101
00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:21.810
<v Maya>apparently.

102
00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:26.270
<v Alex>Seems like it’s linked to investor pressure regarding capital spending, too.

103
00:05:26.690 --> 00:05:29.630
<v Maya>Which, given their negative free cash flow, makes sense.

104
00:05:29.630 --> 00:05:30.290
<v Alex>Exactly.

105
00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:36.330
<v Maya>Apparently Jim Cramer is warming up to CoreWeave, according to CNBC.

106
00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:42.810
<v Maya>Their stock surged nearly 25% after they released earnings and the CEO had a chat

107
00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:43.236
<v Maya>with him.

108
00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:44.070
<v Alex>Cramer?

109
00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:45.590
<v Alex>I thought he’d be more skeptical.

110
00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:49.190
<v Maya>Yeah, he was, but he’s changed his tune.

111
00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:52.110
<v Maya>They’re seeing demand ten times greater than they can supply,

112
00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:54.570
<v Maya>which is allowing them to increase prices.

113
00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:57.430
<v Alex>Makes you wonder how sustainable that is, though.

114
00:05:57.850 --> 00:06:00.070
<v Maya>They’re also dealing with a significant debt load,

115
00:06:00.070 --> 00:06:03.930
<v Maya>which has been weighing down the stock, but the surge helped a bit.

116
00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:08.070
<v Alex>So, they're financing with equipment and contracts now, huh?

117
00:06:08.070 --> 00:06:08.930
<v Alex>Interesting pivot.

118
00:06:09.370 --> 00:06:14.460
<v Maya>Apparently the CEO was tempted to buy back their own bonds on the open market,

119
00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:17.690
<v Maya>but he figures building data centers is the better return.

120
00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:20.210
<v Alex>And the Nvidia chips?

121
00:06:20.210 --> 00:06:22.720
<v Alex>They're holding their value longer than people thought.

122
00:06:24.390 --> 00:06:25.450
<v Alex>That's the news.

123
00:06:25.450 --> 00:06:26.270
<v Alex>Time for The Takeaway.

124
00:06:27.010 --> 00:06:31.850
<v Alex>I just think the satellite stuff—seeing maneuvers in orbit—sounds like a massive

125
00:06:31.850 --> 00:06:34.590
<v Alex>engineering challenge, even if it’s a demo.

126
00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:39.870
<v Maya>I’m going to be watching how quickly those Business Agents for ChatGPT start

127
00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:45.390
<v Maya>impacting actual productivity and, crucially, the bottom line for those businesses.

128
00:06:46.370 --> 00:06:50.810
<v Alex>I'm curious to see if Meta's infrastructure shift really changes anything,

129
00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:53.590
<v Alex>or if it's just a shuffle of deck chairs.

130
